From df4127876e2e5b8a041811063c07423e97ddb5a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:40:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 31/41] tee: qseecom: load the image from firmware, not from
 user space

Loading took the assembled image from the caller as a UBUF, with the kernel
deliberately staying out of firmware layout policy. That is the wrong side of
the trust boundary. QSEE applications run at a higher privilege level than the
kernel, share one application ID space, and reach the same secure storage, so
letting a process choose the bytes that go in there is not something an
"only root can do it" argument covers: the caller still picks which signed
image runs, and the secure world has no notion of which one the kernel meant.

Take the amdtee contract instead. The caller names an application; the driver
fetches <name>.mdt and its .bNN segments with request_firmware() and assembles
them itself. What may be loaded is now whatever the firmware search path holds.
Since the name becomes part of a firmware path, reject one that is empty, too
long, contains a slash, or is "." or "..".

Assembly goes through the new qcom_mdt_read_image(), which produces the
contiguous form QSEE wants, so the format knowledge stays in mdt_loader.c.

This drops the UBUF image and the mdt-length value from the open_session ABI:
loading now takes one parameter, the name.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
